"John H." <Joh...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Kent Paul Dolan <xanth...@well.com> wrote:

> Against it, for balance

Well, current late game lack of "balance" is what
SporkHack is trying to _fix_, so that leaves a lot
of stuff open for review. As Derek responded, the
genocide _is_ one of the "binary" features of
NetHack that might be changed.

> and historical reasons,

That's a bit stronger argument, but the same
counter argument should suffice.

> and because if monsters of some type can still
> show up it's not really -genocide-, is it?

Right, exactly, but that's the change being
suggested -- to do something less than a full
"genocide".

"Genocide" is indeed a logical and conceptual binary
concept, but that's just a _name_, and subject to a
fairly easy  change ("easy" if I don't have to do
the typing).

"Decimate", as usually misunderstood (it _means_
"kill one in ten", but it is usually misunderstood
to mean "kill _all but_ one in ten") would work,
somewhat.

Alternatively, some neologism (an "eniad" is a
"group of nine", so a "enia(di)cide" might suggest
killing nine of ten, or, a "nonacide" from the base
for "nonagon", a nine sided figure).

Whatever, I'm not married to this idea in
particular, just to the general introduction of more
pure evil into NetHack if that's what it takes to
make the late game players still feel "challenged".

